\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The two-site reflection Gram matrix}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take two real field coordinates #{x,y}, interchanged by
reflection, and the centered Gaussian precision
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}a&-w\\-w&a\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad a>|w|.}
The positive-half observables depend on #{x}. The covariance is
##{G=Q^{-1}
 =\frac1{a^2-w^2}\begin{pmatrix}a&w\\w&a\end{pmatrix}.}
For #{a=2,w=1}, this becomes
##{G=\begin{pmatrix}2/3&1/3\\1/3&2/3\end{pmatrix}.}
The energy has positive mass squared #{a-w=1} and crossing weight
#{w=1}, so the same cutoff criterion proves reflection positivity.}

\p{For #{f_0=1,f_1=x,f_2=x^2}, the reflection Gram matrix is
##{\left(B_\mu(f_j,f_k)\right)_{j,k=0}^2
 =\begin{pmatrix}
 1&0&2/3\\
 0&1/3&0\\
 2/3&0&2/3
 \end{pmatrix}.}
Odd Gaussian moments vanish, and
#{\mathbb E[x^2y^2]=(2/3)^2+2(1/3)^2=2/3}
by the [four-factor Gaussian calculation](fdip-005T).
The leading principal minors are #{1}, #{1/3} and #{2/27},
so this finite Gram matrix is positive definite. Positivity of
this one matrix is an illustration; the criterion proves the
claim for all positive-half observables.}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.integral_mul_mul_mul_multivariateGaussian}
proves the fourth-moment identity for a finite coordinate index set
and a positive-semidefinite covariance, including repeated indices.
Apply it with the displayed #{G} and indices #{x,x,y,y}.
This correspondence supports the moment calculation; the reflected
pairing and its Gram-matrix positivity are computed here.}

\p{If instead #{a=2,w=-1}, the precision remains positive definite
and its Gaussian remains reflection invariant. But
##{B_\mu(x,x)=\mathbb E[xy]=-\frac13<0.}
Thus reflection invariance and ordinary Gaussian positivity do not
imply reflection positivity. The witness #{x} is square-integrable;
bounded value truncations also have a negative pairing once the
cutoff is large enough, by continuity of #{B_\mu}.}
